  "articleBody": "It's super frustrating when you've put in all that effort and your drywall seams are still visible, even after painting. This usually points to an issue during the taping and mudding process. A common culprit is not letting each coat of joint compound dry completely. Rushing this step, especially trying to sand or apply another coat too soon, can lead to shrinking or ridges that pop through your paint later. Also, applying the compound too thickly can cause problems, making it harder to get a smooth, even surface. You want thin, consistent layers. Sometimes, folks also skip tape, or use the wrong kind for the job, which can lead to bubbling or cracking. Make sure you’re properly feathering the edges of each mud coat to blend it seamlessly into the existing wall. If you're seeing these issues, it might be worth stepping back and reviewing your technique. For really stubborn seams, or if you're dealing with lots of damage, it might be time to call in a professional. They can often identify subtle issues and correct them before they become major headaches, saving you time and frustration in the long run. Don't hesitate to reach out for a smooth, flawless finish!",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
